## What it is

Microlyte is an FDA 510(k)-cleared antimicrobial wound dressing (K153756). It's an ultrathin, conforming matrix that molds to the wound bed's micro-contours, maintains a moist healing environment that supports granulation, and delivers sustained ionic-silver antimicrobial activity (per the manufacturer's in vitro testing, 99.99% microbial reduction sustained beyond 72 hours). It is fully bioresorbable — typically within about a week, so there's no removal step — and because it's entirely synthetic, there are no animal-derived components and no refrigeration or special storage.

The practical appeal: it lets you manage wound bioburden and support healing in a single, easy-to-apply product. In the published chronic-wound study, Microlyte replaced patients' prior antimicrobial dressings and topical antibiotics.

## The published evidence

*(from the Microlyte platform — the Matrix and SURGICAL configurations)*

- **Chronic wounds** — *"Efficacy of a Bioresorbable Matrix in Healing Complex Chronic Wounds: An Open-Label Prospective Pilot Study."* Wounds. 2020;32(11):309–318. Across 32 patients (35 wounds — venous stasis ulcers, diabetic foot ulcers, postoperative and burn wounds, and chronic lower-extremity ulcers), **91% of wounds (29/32) healed completely or improved significantly by 12 weeks, with a 73% average reduction in wound area.**

- **Surgical site infection prevention** — Chatelain R. Wounds. 2021;33(10):245–252. In a prospective clinical case series of at-risk diabetic patients undergoing foot and ankle surgery, prophylactic Microlyte SURGICAL was associated with **no surgical site infections in the per-protocol group, against an approximately 13% historical baseline.** This is a small, single-arm case series — worth reviewing in full before presenting to a P&T or formulary committee.

## How it's covered under NGS

Microlyte matrix bills under **HCPCS A2005** (per square centimeter); application is reported with **CPT 15271–15278**.

In the **NGS (National Government Services)** jurisdiction there is **no Local Coverage Determination** governing skin substitutes/CTPs. Coverage is therefore determined directly under the statute — **Section 1862(a)(1)(A) of the Social Security Act**, which covers items and services that are "reasonable and necessary for the diagnosis or treatment of illness or injury." Because there is no LCD in force, there is no fixed prerequisite to clear first (no mandated four-week failed-conservative-care window, no application cap).

Practically, that means Microlyte can be considered **early in the wound-care course — during conservative management — when antimicrobial control is clinically indicated**, provided the note establishes medical necessity under the §1862(a)(1)(A) standard. Worth being clear-eyed about one point: absent an LCD, coverage is a case-by-case determination on that statutory standard, so the documentation is what carries the claim.

## Documenting medical necessity (§1862(a)(1)(A))

Because coverage rests on "reasonable and necessary" rather than an LCD checklist, the clinical note does the work. A defensible note should:

1. **Identify** the presence — or risk — of microbial contamination or elevated bioburden at the wound site.
2. **State** that Microlyte® was applied to reduce wound bioburden through its sustained ionic-silver antimicrobial activity.
3. **Record** the clinical rationale for selecting an antimicrobial matrix over a non-antimicrobial alternative.
4. **Tie the use to the label** — note that the application was consistent with the FDA-cleared indication (510(k) K153756) and performed per the manufacturer's Instructions for Use (IFU). On-label use per the IFU reinforces that the product was applied as intended and supports the "reasonable and necessary" determination.

The **510(k) clearance** and the **IFU** are both in the attached package precisely so the note can reference them: the 510(k) establishes the cleared antimicrobial indication, and the IFU establishes correct on-label application.

